{
  "$comment": "The hook decision table. One row per enforcement decision, as DATA. Adding a case is one object, not one more hand-written assertion. Every row pins BOTH halves of the decision: what the host was told (permissionDecision) AND what the ledger recorded (verdict + rule). Pinning only the first is how a guard that stops firing goes unnoticed; pinning only a deny is how an allow that stops being INSPECTED goes unnoticed. The `rule` on an allow row is the load-bearing part: it proves the hook looked and cleared, rather than never having run. A row may carry `setup`: files written into the workspace before the hook fires, which is what lets one table walk an entire defer ladder rung by rung instead of testing only its outermost rung. NOTE: projectRoot() accepts .shapeup/active-scope, a committed shapeup/ tier, or .git as a root marker — a bare .shapeup/ directory is deliberately NOT one, so every setup that wants the workspace treated as the project root must create active-scope.",
  "hooks": {
    "safety-spine": {
      "argv": [
        "hooks/safety-spine.mjs"
      ],
      "event": "PreToolUse"
    },
    "sandbox-guard": {
      "argv": [
        "hooks/sandbox-guard.mjs"
      ],
      "event": "PreToolUse"
    },
    "gate-intake": {
      "argv": [
        "hooks/gate-intake.mjs"
      ],
      "event": "PreToolUse"
    },
    "validate-envelope": {
      "argv": [
        "kernel/harness.mjs",
        "verify",
        "envelope"
      ],
      "event": "PreToolUse"
    },
    "dispatch-receipt": {
      "argv": [
        "hooks/dispatch-receipt.mjs"
      ],
      "event": "PostToolUse"
    },
    "gate-zerowork": {
      "argv": [
        "hooks/gate-zerowork.mjs"
      ],
      "event": "Stop"
    }
  },
  "rows": [
